⟦900⟧{{smaller block|''The Relations between Displacement, Compression and Velocity''.—The relations shown by figs. 4 and 5 in a general manner may easily be put into exact form. Let <math>\text{OX}</math> (fig. 6) be the direction
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{sc|Fig.}} 6.}}
of travel, and let <math>x</math> be the distance of any point <math>\text{M}</math> from a fixed point <math>\text{O}</math>. Let <math>\text{ON} = x + dx</math>. Let <math>\text{MP} = y</math> represent the forward displacement of the particle originally at <math>\text{M}</math>, and <math>\text{NQ} = y + dy</math> that of the particle originally at <math>\text{N}</math>. The layer of air originally of thickness <math>dx</math> now has thickness <math>dx + dy</math>, since <math>\text{N}</math> is displaced forwards <math>dy</math> more than <math>\text{M}</math>. The volume <math>dx</math>, then, has increased to <math>dx + dy</math> or volume <math>1</math> has increased to <math>1 + dy/dx</math> and the increase of volume <math>1</math> is <math>dy/dx</math>.}}

⟦1500⟧{{smaller block|Let <math>\text{E}</math> be the bulk modulus of elasticity, defined as increase of pressure <math>\div</math> decrease of volume per unit volume where the pressure increase is so small that this ratio is constant, <math>\omega</math> the small increase of pressure, and <math>-(dy/dx)</math> the volume ''decrease'', then
{{block center|<math>\text{E} = \omega/(-dy/dx)</math> or <math>\omega /\text{E} = -dy/dx</math> (1)}}
This gives the relation between pressure excess and displacement.}}

⟦1700⟧{{smaller block|To find the relation of the velocity to displacement and pressure we shall express the fact that the wave travels on carrying all its conditions with it, so that the displacement now at <math>\text{M}</math> will arrive at <math>\text{N}</math> while the wave travels over <math>\text{MN}</math>. Let <math>\text{U}</math> be the velocity of the wave and let <math>u</math> be the velocity of the particle originally at <math>\text{N}</math>. Let <math>\text{MN} = dx = \text{U}dt</math>. In the time <math>dt</math> which the wave takes to travel over <math>\text{MN}</math> the particle displacement at <math>\text{N}</math> changes by <math>\text{QR}</math>, and <math>\text{QR} = -udt</math>, so that <math>\text{QR}/\text{MN} = -u/\text{U}</math>. But <math>\text{QR}/\text{MN} = dy/dx</math>. Then
{{block center|<math>u/\text{U} = -dy/dx</math> (2)}}
This gives the velocity of any particle in terms of the displacement. Equating (1) and (2)
{{block center|<math>u/\text{U} = \omega/\text{E}</math> (3)}}
which gives the particle velocity in terms of the pressure excess.}}

⟦2100⟧Generally, if any condition <math>\phi</math> in the wave is carried forward unchanged with velocity <math>\text{U}</math>, the change of <math>\phi</math> at a given point in time <math>dt</math> is equal to the change of <math>\phi</math> as we go ''back'' along the curve a distance <math>dx = \text{U}dt</math> at the beginning of <math>dt</math>.
{{block center|Then <math>\frac{d\phi}{dx} = -\frac{1}{\text{U}}\frac{d\phi}{dt}.</math>}}

⟦2300⟧''The Characteristics of Sound Waves Corresponding to Loudness, Pitch and Quality''.—Sounds differ from each other only in the three respects of loudness, pitch and quality.

⟦2500⟧The ''loudness'' of the sound brought by a train of waves of given wave-length depends on the extent of the to and fro excursion of the air particles. This is obvious if we consider that the greater the vibration of the source the greater is the excursion of the air in the issuing waves, and the louder is the sound heard. Half the total excursion is called the amplitude. Thus in fig. 4 <math>\text{QJ}</math> is the amplitude. Methods of measuring the amplitude in sound waves in air have been devised and will be described later. We may say here that the energy or the intensity of the sound of given wave-length is proportional to the square of the amplitude.

